<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freyja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Freya, Freyia, Freja, Gefn, Hörn, Mardöll, Sýr, Vanadís, Valfreyja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Norse mythology Freyja, whose name means lady in Old Norse, is a goddess associated with love, beauty, fertility, war, gold and seiðr, a magic for seeing and influencing the future. A member of the Vanir with her twin brother Freyr and her father Njörðr, she owns the necklace Brísingamen, rides a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> pulled by two cats and receives half of those who die in battle in her field Fólkvangr. She is attested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Poetic Edda</a>, in the Prose Edda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>, in sagas and in skaldic poetry, and rural Scandinavians still acknowledged her into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freyja.jpg" alt="Freyja" width="330" height="557" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Doyle Penrose (1862-1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freyja_and_the_Necklace_by_James_Doyle_Penrose,_1890.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vanir were gods of fertility and prosperity: Njord, god of the sea and wealth, and his children Freyr, who brought peace and good harvests, and <b>Freyja</b>, goddess of love and of seidr, a form of magic, who received half of those slain in battle.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…and kings, who held sacrificial feasts, blót, at their halls and at sacred places, and the gods were many, among them Odin, god of wisdom, poetry and the dead in battle, Thor, the thunderer who guarded gods and people, and Freyr and <b>Freyja</b>, who gave fertility and plenty.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
